      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Despite their importance and wide application, the quality of meta-analyses is highly variable [1,7,10,11]. If the quality of meta-analyses is poor, it is hard to know if 'biological metaanalysis embodies 'mega-enlightenment', a 'mega-mistake', or something in between' [12]. One issue that can prevent readers from evaluating the overall quality of a published metaanalysis is the lack of details describing each step in the meta-analysis.",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the current state of knowledge regarding the quality of ecological meta-analyses, noting that while they are widely applied, their quality is highly variable and often lacks sufficient detail."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Assessing the current compliance with recommended steps for reporting and execution in ecological meta-analyses and identifying places that need improvements can help guide the meta-analytic community towards more robust inference and reduce controversy.",
      "reason": "The authors explain the need for the review, stating that assessing compliance with reporting and execution standards will help guide the community toward more robust inference."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "First, we compiled information from 18 studies in the last 20 years (between 2002 and 2022) that reviewed the quality of meta-analyses in ecology, evolution and related fields. ... Second, we evaluated the recognition and treatment of non-independence for the ecological meta-analyses included in Pappalardo et al. [1], and extracted new data on quality criteria to compare it with the other reviews.",
      "reason": "The manuscript acknowledges existing review papers (18 studies) and explains that this review compiles and expands upon their findings, specifically re-evaluating data from Pappalardo et al. [1]."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is a meta-evaluation (a review of reviews) of ecological meta-analyses, not a review of a specific intervention effect; therefore, a mechanism of action is not applicable."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is a meta-evaluation (a review of reviews) of ecological meta-analyses, not a review of a complex intervention or delivery context; therefore, a logic model is not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "In this paper, we reviewed the literature assessing the quality of ecological meta-analyses, collected new data to evaluate current practices, and highlight the areas that need more work. First, we compiled information from 18 studies in the last 20 years (between 2002 and 2022) that reviewed the quality of meta-analyses in ecology, evolution and related fields. These papers provided different insights on the compliance with different standards of reporting quality and with recommended execution steps that should be part of a meta-analysis. Second, we evaluated the recognition and treatment of non-independence for the ecological meta-analyses included in Pappalardo et al. [1], and extracted new data on quality criteria to compare it with the other reviews. Finally, we summarized the level of compliance for different quality criteria across these 18 previous meta-analysis reviews and the new data taken from papers reviewed in Pappalardo et al [1].",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly states the objectives of the review in the Introduction section, detailing the three main components of the study: reviewing existing literature, evaluating new data on non-independence, and summarizing compliance levels."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is a meta-evaluation (a review of reviews) of the quality of reporting and execution in ecological meta-analyses, not a review of intervention effects. Therefore, the requirement to state planned comparisons using PICO is not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

